Puppet, but simpler.

- Unlike Puppet: No server (other than a Git remote)
- Unlike Ansible: No manual runs

A systemd service that runs every 30 minutes and:

1. "Pulls" (really fetch + hard reset) a repo.
2. Figures out the definitions that apply to it (via manually set /etc/muppet/muppetclasses)
3. Applies changes

The latter part is the hard one: I want no DSL, (almost) no magic.

Definitions are shell scripts that get executed if the muppetclasses match it.

Muppet also adds a bunch of little helper executables to build the declarative fundamentals, e.g.:

- ensure-file some/file /at/a/place --permissions 755 --owner root
- ensure-files some/folder /at/a/place --permissions 755 --owner root
- ensure-folder some/file /at/a/place
- ensure-user somename
- ...

Source files are by convention relative to the muppetclass root or something
like that. There's also an env var pointing there so you can also use it in
custom stuff.

Generally, the ensure-... functionality should:

- Run quickly when it's already the case
- Absolutely make the thing happen, whatever that entails.

Definitions _should_ be written without unnecessary executions of stuff, since
this will run every 30 minutes or so.

Open questions:

What language do we write all this in?

- Python?
    - Probably always there, at least _some_ python3. But which one do we depend on...
    - Probably best to use only stdlib, no packages, and fairly old language features...
    - Won't be in minimal docker images etc., but the target is anyways _host_ machines.
    - Most familiar in, will get stuff working quickly
- Rust? Go? Nim? Zig?
    - Advantage: Static* binary that just works. (* possible with Rust afaik, but needs to be compiled specially)
- Shell?
    - Which one? sh only? bash?
    - Fairly limited functionality, simple things are hard

How do we deploy/package muppet itself?

- Via muppet! :D
- Debian package?
- Git checkout with init script or something like that?
- How about:
    - Script in repo to automatically SSH, copy things over, set up systemd service...


Structure of a muppetconfig repo:

/recipes
    /nginx
        muppetfile
        nginx.conf
    /page123
        muppetfile
        configs/
            file1
            file2
